    "description": "Iran’s accommodation landscape includes contemporary hotels and historic buildings adapted for overnight stays. These properties illuminate different aspects of travel infrastructure, from room services and booking arrangements to the conservation of older architecture.",
    "tags": ["Hotels", "Accommodation", "Hospitality", "Historic Buildings", "Travel Infrastructure"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:53:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:53:56Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Hotels in Iran: Contemporary Hospitality and Historic Accommodation",
      "description": "Explore contemporary hotel services, historic accommodation and booking distinctions in Iran, with clearly identified evidence gaps."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-accommodation-experiences",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different buildings, different experiences",
        "content": "Hotels provide a useful way to understand the relationship between Iranian tourism and the built environment. A contemporary property can organize its offer around room categories, restaurants and event facilities; a converted historic building makes its architecture part of the visitor experience. These are different accommodation models, and neither a modern appearance nor a historic setting independently establishes service quality. The inspected examples illustrate this range rather than representing a national ranking. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nEspinas Palace in Tehran presents a contemporary model. Its official room catalogue distinguishes standard rooms, family rooms and suites, specifying features such as balconies and city or mountain views. The practical distinction is the room actually reserved: photographs of a prominent suite do not describe every room in the building. The catalogue establishes what the operator advertises, while independent inspection would be needed to assess maintenance, accessibility and consistency of service. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historic-hospitality",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Historic hospitality and adaptive reuse",
        "content": "A caravanserai was an inn serving people moving along a travel route. UNESCO explains that these establishments supplied shelter, food and water, and that their locations reflected water availability, geography and security. Their architectural variation shows how accommodation responded to different environments. This history helps explain why an old lodging building can be culturally significant beyond its present commercial use. [s2]\n\nThe Abbasi Hotel in Isfahan illustrates the reuse of a caravanserai as a hotel. ToIran’s architectural account describes rooms arranged around a large courtyard, subsequently landscaped as a garden during the building’s conversion. This is a useful example of adaptive reuse: an older structure acquires a new operation while retaining a recognizable spatial organization. The account supports that architectural explanation; it does not establish the hotel’s current conservation condition or independently verify its services. [s3]\n\nUNESCO’s Persian Caravanserai listing documents a selected heritage property. It should not be read as a hotel directory or as evidence that every historic inn welcomes overnight guests. Heritage significance and present accommodation availability require separate verification. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "booking-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Booking arrangements and the limits of this overview",
        "content": "The booking channel can affect who handles a reservation problem. On its website, Espinas distinguishes direct reservations from those made through an online travel agency and assigns responsibility for agency bookings to that intermediary. This is the operator’s stated position, rather than an independent determination of a guest’s contractual rights. It nevertheless shows why the identity of the seller matters alongside the identity of the hotel. [s4]\n\nThe same company lists properties in Tehran and Astara, in Gilan Province, demonstrating that even one operator can work across different destinations. Those examples cannot establish the national balance between city, coastal, pilgrimage and heritage accommodation. A fuller account needs an authoritative accommodation register, the applicable classification rules and comparable statistics. Until those are inspected, this page offers a grounded introduction to contrasting hotel models rather than a comprehensive assessment of Iran’s hotel sector. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
