    "description": "Travel agencies and tour operators connect visitors with accommodation, transport, guides and organized itineraries in Iran. Their advertised services overlap, but tourism operations, passenger ticketing and pilgrimage travel have distinct regulatory histories and should not be treated as interchangeable credentials.",
    "tags": ["Travel Agencies", "Tour Operators", "Inbound Tourism", "Travel Services", "Tourism Regulation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:53:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:53:56Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Travel Agencies in Iran: Services, Tour Operators and Credentials",
      "description": "Understand how Iranian travel agencies assemble tours, how services differ and why licensing and accreditation need separate verification."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "assembling-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connecting the parts of a journey",
        "content": "An agency can provide a connection between a visitor and several different suppliers. Accommodation, transfers, guides and transport reservations may be assembled into one itinerary rather than purchased separately. Iran Doostan’s company introduction, for example, advertises hotel bookings, transfers, flights, multilingual guides and both package and customized tours. This establishes the company’s stated range of services; it does not independently verify delivery or customer satisfaction. [s1]\n\nIts advertised specialisms include cultural, adventure and nature travel, as well as MICE: meetings, incentives, conferences and exhibitions. These categories show that an operator’s work can extend beyond a conventional sightseeing holiday. A business event and a cultural itinerary may require different arrangements even when the same company markets both. The relevant distinction is the scope of the contracted service, rather than the breadth of a company’s promotional list. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "agency-and-operator",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A booking service or an organized itinerary?",
        "content": "The boundary between selling a component and organizing a journey is visible on agency websites. Iran Safar presents separate hotel and flight search functions alongside cultural, desert, climbing, skiing and customized tours. Its service menu also includes guides, airport transfers and travel insurance. A single website can therefore combine reservation functions with the marketing of more extensive itineraries. The presence of those functions does not demonstrate successful payment, confirmed availability or insurance coverage for a particular traveler. [s2]\n\nFor readers interpreting an offer, the useful questions are concrete: which services are included, which supplier provides them and which business handles changes? These are editorial questions prompted by the overlapping services in the inspected operator descriptions. They should be resolved in the actual offer and contract rather than inferred from a tour title or a website menu. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regulatory-distinctions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different activities, different credentials",
        "content": "Iran’s regulation on travel-service offices, adopted in Solar Hijri year 1380, distinguishes passenger travel services, tourism services and pilgrimage services. Its opening provisions describe ticket sales and passenger transport arrangements separately from activities such as visa arrangements and domestic or international group tours. A further category concerns pilgrimage travel. This historical framework explains why the everyday English word “agency” can conceal different authorized activities. [s3]\n\nThe inspected text is a legal republication, rather than a verified current official register. Its original references to government bodies cannot safely establish which institution exercises each responsibility at the editorial cutoff. Likewise, the regulation’s existence does not verify an individual business’s license. A current account requires an authoritative consolidated text and a functioning mechanism for checking the particular office and activity. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "accreditation-and-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Accreditation is a separate question",
        "content": "The International Air Transport Association, or IATA, maintains an Iran-specific travel-agent accreditation requirements page. Its checklist includes company and aviation-related documentation. This is evidence of a distinct airline-industry accreditation process, not proof that every Iranian tour operator participates in it or needs it for every service. [s4]\n\nAn accreditation claim, a tourism license and an advertised itinerary answer different questions. None alone establishes the quality of a journey. This overview therefore explains the distinctions without endorsing an operator, confirming current visa rules or presenting an unverified agency directory. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
